<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>Standing performance guards: the engine's marshalling and evaluation paths, and the canvas spatial index.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="BenchmarkDotNet" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Spark.Host brings the engine, the node library and the geometry kernel in one reference,
    which is the same door the application and the CLI come through. Spark.UI is here for
    SceneIndex alone - the canvas's retained spatial index is pure managed data-structure
    code, and it is the substrate ADR-0013's whole claim rests on.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Spark.Host\Spark.Host.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Spark.UI\Spark.UI.csproj" />

    <!--
      The OpenCascade provider, for the `tessellate` verb. A Brep cannot be tessellated without one,
      and that verb exists because nothing else in bench/ measures the path between an evaluated
      solid and a mesh - which is where E12-T19's eighteen seconds hid.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Spark.Geometry.Occt\Spark.Geometry.Occt.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
